+# Aesthetics Perspective
+
+## Artifact Summary
+
+What: Codex readability and style evaluation of T3 iter2's amended `orchestration/SKILL.md`. Why: this workflow contract must be scannable by a future manager, especially around the superseded lock and the Chat gate list. How: close-read the updated prose around Orchestration Mode, Inter-loop transition, Workflow State Machine, and Mode-specific gates, then verify count wording and link density with grep.
+
+Memory reads: prompt `codex-eval-prompt-iter2.md`; target `orchestration/SKILL.md`; `chat-mode.md`; `auto-mode.md`; worktree Idea and Plan artifacts; main-tree iter1 Codex files; project mistakes on section order, docs-sync whole-file grep, and verification-without-evidence; `evaluation/SKILL.md`; `execution/evaluation.md`. I did not read or write `session.json`.
+
+## Locked Frame (Stage 1)
+
+Scenario A1: The supersession is visually obvious without deleting the old contract. Checklist: old lock sentence is still present; strikethrough marks supersession; nearby CORRECTION text explains where to read the replacement.
+
+Scenario A2: Gate prose is internally polished. Checklist: lead-in count matches the table row count; the fourth gate is named plainly; options are readable.
+
+Scenario A3 (adversarial): Link-heavy text becomes noisy or hides the main contract. Checklist: inline Chat/Auto summaries remain one sentence each; sub-doc links carry detail rather than duplicating the full specs in SKILL.md.
+
+## Stage 2 Findings
+
